  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0013_b4ef37b552e5.png\" style=\"width: 446px; height: 199px;\" /></p><p>This structure represents sucrose in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>-</mo></math>glucose and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>-</mo></math>fructose is attached to each other by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> glycosidic linkage. Since reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, this is considered as non-reducing sugar.</p>"
